(6645591, 990787, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>First impression: ''Wow do I ever want to get this shirt and tie off!''  Glad to say I gave this a 6, because it does convey a lot more than many portraits, which are usually flattering to the subject. <br/> <br/>Artistic: Very raw and convincing self-portrait, you went for the stressed-out look so well i can feel you vibrating from here.  Either you''re about to attend a year-end review with the head honcho (and didn''t get a good night''s sleep or enough time to shave or comb your hair), or you had about 10 cups of espresso pre-shoot :-)  I think you managed to hit on the universal theme of discomfort and tenseness with this shot.  Pity more voters didn''t relate to that end of the human spectrum of emotions.<br/> <br/>Technical: Very good in terms of lighting, the comp is certainly more compelling than the usual eyes-to-the-camera which you see in most portraits.  Looks like you may have been a little close to the bg but then, maybe that''s what stressed you out? ;-) I do like the lighting descrip and never apologize for homemade low-budget light setups.  Besides, the lighting adds to the impression that you are feeling the heat.<br/> <br/>Overall: Excellent work and not a bad finish at all, so give yourself a pat on the back.  And always nice to see a shout-out to teammates who''ve given you help along the way.  Keep up the shooting and exploring those emotions so rarely seen here!<br/> <br/>Feel free to PM me.<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! First impression: ''Wow do I ever want to get this shirt and tie off!''  Glad to say I gave this a 6, because it does convey a lot more than many portraits, which are usually flattering to the subject.  Artistic: Very raw and convincing self-portrait, you went for the stressed-out look so well i can feel you vibrating from here.  Either you''re about to attend a year-end review with the head honcho (and didn''t get a good night''s sleep or enough time to shave or comb your hair), or you had about 10 cups of espresso pre-shoot :-)  I think you managed to hit on the universal theme of discomfort and tenseness with this shot.  Pity more voters didn''t relate to that end of the human spectrum of emotions. Technical: Very good in terms of lighting, the comp is certainly more compelling than the usual eyes-to-the-camera which you see in most portraits.  Looks like you may have been a little close to the bg but then, maybe that''s what stressed you out? ;-) I do like the lighting descrip and never apologize for homemade low-budget light setups.  Besides, the lighting adds to the impression that you are feeling the heat. Overall: Excellent work and not a bad finish at all, so give yourself a pat on the back.  And always nice to see a shout-out to teammates who''ve given you help along the way.  Keep up the shooting and exploring those emotions so rarely seen here! Feel free to PM me. Susan', '2012-01-15 17:49:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(6645774, 989744, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>First impression: Cartoony Tim Burton-esque shot, and looks like someone had a lot of fun getting ready for the shoot!<br/> <br/>Artistic: Love the hair/makeup and expression, all so priceless, and the gloves and boa just adds to the impact further heightened by the scarlet bg. <br/> <br/>Technical: Straightforward, head-on shots are amongst the most challenging, so good work on the lighting.  In terms of comp I think it''s great with the gloves framing the boa which lead up to the face, which in turn leads to the hair and those big features :-) Lighting looks pretty good.  A slightly longer exposure or higher ISO may have helped bleach you out a little more to add to the impact, but maybe you already tried that.  I have the Nikon version of that lens and it''s a great one, keep it!<br/> <br/>Overall: Very good work, good finish for what is probably one of your first challenges here.  Keep up the creativity and shooting, and hope to see more of your work soon.<br/> <br/>Feel free to PM me.<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! First impression: Cartoony Tim Burton-esque shot, and looks like someone had a lot of fun getting ready for the shoot! Artistic: Love the hair/makeup and expression, all so priceless, and the gloves and boa just adds to the impact further heightened by the scarlet bg.  Technical: Straightforward, head-on shots are amongst the most challenging, so good work on the lighting.  In terms of comp I think it''s great with the gloves framing the boa which lead up to the face, which in turn leads to the hair and those big features :-) Lighting looks pretty good.  A slightly longer exposure or higher ISO may have helped bleach you out a little more to add to the impact, but maybe you already tried that.  I have the Nikon version of that lens and it''s a great one, keep it! Overall: Very good work, good finish for what is probably one of your first challenges here.  Keep up the creativity and shooting, and hope to see more of your work soon. Feel free to PM me. Susan', '2012-01-15 21:37:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(6647430, 990724, 93565, '<td valign="top">One of the more genuine expressions in the challenge, which I think is one of the most important yet difficult aspects of portrait photography. Of course, it''s easier when you''re the subject to do that, but boy is it hard to trip that shutter sometimes....<br/>I like that you went and did something you''re supposed to avoid with portraits (wide angle up very close), it gives an interesting feel to things.</td>', 'One of the more genuine expressions in the challenge, which I think is one of the most important yet difficult aspects of portrait photography. Of course, it''s easier when you''re the subject to do that, but boy is it hard to trip that shutter sometimes....I like that you went and did something you''re supposed to avoid with portraits (wide angle up very close), it gives an interesting feel to things.', '2012-01-17 07:57:18.000000', NULL, 0),
